                  <provenance type="#e" source="5149"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_487">Sawley,
                        Yorkshire. Cistercian abbey of St Mary the Virgin</orgName><p>Fol. ii recto,
                        erased but clearly legible by u. v. light: <q>Liber sancte Marie de
                           Salleia</q>. Information from C. R. Cheney, July 1962.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance cert="low" source="5626"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_579"
                        >Westminster, Middlesex. Benedictine abbey of St Peter</orgName><p>Formerly
                        at Sawley abbey, identified by ex libris. Almost certainly the book seen at
                        Westminster by John Bale (1495–1563), who listed a series of rare and unique
                        works, all carried by this manuscript, in his <title>Index</title>, with the
                        reference <q>Ex coenobio Westmonasteriensi</q> or <q>Ex libro
                           Westmonasteriensi</q> (<title>Index</title>, 13, 348, 407, 409, 419,
                        461). It is not known how the book came to be transferred to Westminster,
                        nor is it certain that Bale saw the book there before dissolution of the
                        house in 1540.</p><p>The manuscript was also very fully described by a
                        rather later writer in a Wrest Park manuscript (<title>HMC 2nd
                           Report</title>, no. 19) under the heading <q>Ex quodam antiquo volumine
                           Westmonasteriensi</q>.</p><p>John Joscelyn wrote that Pekyns, prebendary
                        of Westminster, had Simeon of Durham and John and Richard of Hexham,
                        probably this manuscript.</p><p/></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_254">Hexham,
                        Northumberland, Augustinian priory of St Andrew</orgName>
                     <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of
                        Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The
                        corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card records the
                        following information, which was deemed unsatisfactory for an attribution:
                        See M. R. James Corpus Christi college catalogue, I, p. 232 (evidence of
                        contents).</p></provenance>
